Summary: six fast paths along the route a numeric token takes

G: six fast paths along the route a numeric token takes

One idea applied six times, in order along a single route: each takes work out of
a step without changing what the step means. Rendering `yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss`
walks all six per value, and none of them is worth filing alone — two clear the
noise floor on V8 only, one on JavaScriptCore only, one on neither. Together they
clear it on both by a wide margin, which is the case for one patch rather than
six.

  formatter.js  num()        a formatter whose numbering system is latn can pad
                             digits itself; PolyNumberFormatter's Intl branch is
                             only needed by the ones that cannot.
  formatter.js  parseFormat  splitting a format string into tokens is pure, and
                             the same few strings recur, so the split is cached
                             rather than redone per value.
  formatter.js  token loop   the loop rebuilt its own state per token; hoisting
                             what does not vary keeps the switch monomorphic.
  util.js       roundTo      every rounding mode is the identity on an integer
                             once digits is non-negative, and an integer is what
                             every numeric token arrives as.
  util.js       padStart     month, day, hour, minute and second all pad to two
                             digits, so a "00".."99" table covers the common
                             case without building either string.
  datetime.js   tsToObj      integer civil math in place of a wrapper Date and
                             seven getter calls. TimeClip is written out here
                             because the Date constructor used to run it: without
                             the truncation a fractional timestamp rendered as
                             "00:00:01.0.5", and without the range check a plus
                             that overflowed answered +287168-08-24 where luxon
                             says invalid. Both are reachable from ordinary
                             calls; see the note at the end.

Two of the six are engine-specific, and they are the weakest part of this patch:
the token loop pays off on V8 and gives a little back on JSC, and the padStart
table is the reverse. They are in because the other four carry the change, and
splitting by engine would mean filing six patches to land four.

K subsumes two of them by construction — parseFormat and the punctuation half of
the token loop — so a tree that already has K gets less from that half of this
patch than the list above suggests.

Only tsToObj is off the formatter, and it is the one that reaches furthest:
every DateTime that reads a calendar field goes through it, so it is under
construction as well as formatting. That is why this patch moves a column like
`millis`, which formats nothing at all.

One correction, not an optimization. Writing the verification sweep turned up two
bugs in the tsToObj rewrite, both from the same cause: replacing new Date(ts)
with civil math also dropped the TimeClip the Date constructor was running on the
way in, which used to truncate the timestamp toward zero and return NaN outside
+/-MAX_DATE. Without the truncation,
DateTime.fromSeconds(1.0005).toISO() rendered "1970-01-01T00:00:01.0.5Z", a
malformed string rather than a merely different one, and fromMillis(-0.5) moved a
whole day, because Date rounds toward zero and Math.floor does not. Without the
range check, fromMillis(0).plus({ milliseconds: 9e15 }).toISO() answered
"+287168-08-24T16:00:00.000Z" where luxon says null — calendar-unit overflow is
caught by the Date.UTC inside objToLocalTS, but the milliseconds H adds are added
to the timestamp after that, so nothing was checking it.

Neither was reachable from any benchmark, which is why both survived: every
timestamp a benchmark builds is a whole number well inside the range. Both are
reachable from ordinary calls. TimeClip is now written out at the top of tsToObj,
and both cases are in the test sweep.

Verification is in benchmarks/test/numeric-path-patch.test.ts, which checks
tsToObj against Date's own getters over 200k random instants across the full
range, and in the --verify pass of benchmarks/format.ts and
benchmarks/upstream.ts, which compares every rendered string against stock.

